# TODO

Long-running work, in the order chosen. Check items off as they land; keep the notes short.

## 1. Disclosure-time pipeline

The edge is speed from disclosure, so disclosure time is the one timestamp everywhere.

- [x] Live discovery: EDGAR current-filings Atom feed (`action=getcurrent&type=4&owner=only&output=atom`, de-duplicated by accession, exact form match) replaces the Selenium scraper; verified live
- [x] Retire Selenium: remove `EdgarClient.fetch_live_links` / `_configure_driver`, the `edgar-live` extra (selenium, beautifulsoup4) and the Chromium layer of the master-index image; update the `Filings` port
- [x] Single timestamp: the scraper parses `ACCEPTANCE-DATETIME` from each filing header and stamps every transaction with it, in backfill and live mode alike
- [x] master-index: drop `received_now`; discovery (quarterly master index or Atom feed) only selects which filings to fetch
- [x] Scraper age filter compares filing publication times (was a no-op in live mode)
- [x] Look-ahead bias: `compute_target` enters at the first price after acceptance (same-day close in session, next open otherwise), not at the trade date; `compute_RT4` only averages labels whose exit close printed before acceptance; every label changes, so BT4/RT4 are rebuilt
- [x] Backfill de-duplication: the master index lists a filing once per filer; master-index keeps one row per accession, and transaction keys use the accession (not the filer-dependent link), so backfill and live discovery agree
- [ ] Backfill throughput: `EdgarClient` caps requests (8/s by default, SEC allows 10 per IP) and waits only the remainder of the gap; a sequential scrape ran at ~8.5 filings/s at the 10/s cap (was ~2/s with fixed sleeps), about 8-10 h for a year of ~250k unique Form 4s. Concurrency adds little beyond the cap; on AWS shard by quarter with a shared rate budget; add progress logging; start with 2-3 years of history
- [ ] SEC errors and rate limits: a 403/429/503 reads as a filing without XML, so the scraper skips it and still commits its offset (the filing is lost); retry with backoff on non-200 and stop before the commit when retries run out. Needed by the concurrent backfill. Observed: a full 1,117-filing scrape at 10 requests/s lost 5 filings (53 transactions) this way; all parsed fine when retried
- [ ] Auction execution (decided: daily granularity): live orders go to the auction the backtest enters at, market-on-open (`opg`) for filings accepted outside the session, market-on-close (`cls`) for in-session ones; replaces today's `market`/`gtc` on run; verify Alpaca's submission windows for `opg`/`cls` before relying on them; lands with the live scorer
- [ ] AWS: monthly market-cap refresh as a scheduled job (EventBridge -> Lambda on the `MarketData` port, snapshot stamped at fetch time) writing the reference table on S3/Iceberg; market-cap snapshots end 2024-08 until then
- [ ] AWS plan: event-driven live path (EventBridge poll -> dedupe -> SQS + DLQ -> scorer Lambda -> Alpaca) beside the batch path (backfill, RT4 labelling, training)

## 2. Research: does the signal survive honest evaluation? (after the data backfill)

- [ ] Walk-forward evaluation: retrain over rolling time windows, collect out-of-sample predictions, report returns after per-trade costs and hedged against SPY (market move removed), next to the naive baselines
- [ ] Long side: insider purchases (where the literature finds the effect); `ACQUIRED_DISPOSED` filter, relaxed sell-only check, buy orders in inference
- [ ] Informative events, one feature at a time: non-10b5-1 trades, several insiders in a window, share of holding traded, first trade in years
- [ ] Shortability: skip names Alpaca won't short (`shortable` / `easy_to_borrow`); only current borrow status is available, a stated backtest limitation
- [ ] Paper trading on Alpaca for several months before any real money

## 3. Backlog (active)

- [x] Training: chronological, purged train/test split (test = latest filings; train keeps labels known before it)
- [x] api-enricher retired: market cap (latest monthly snapshot before acceptance), exchange, SIC and SIC group added in target (`add_company_context`) from `mcaps.parquet` and `companies.json`
- [ ] SIC straight from the filing header (`STANDARD INDUSTRIAL CLASSIFICATION`) for companies missing from `companies.json`
- [x] CI: GitHub Actions running `make quality-static` (blocking) and `type-check` (non-blocking); unverified until pushed
- [ ] mypy: last 6 errors are the Kafka client (`messaging.py`); goes with the SQS migration, then `type-check` joins the pre-commit hook
- [x] Unit tests per project (81): Form 4 parsing on real fixtures (10b5-1, joint filings, holdings, keys, tickers), filing messages both ways, price refresh and rebasing, next print, disclosure aggregation and clusters, valuation, labels and their safeguards, known-label mean; shared typed fakes with mypy conformance; `make test-unit` in the gate
- [ ] Tests still to write: kafka-to-store cleaning, training (split, evaluate, model save/load), inference (scoring, order placement); integration tier against a temporary Iceberg catalog
- [ ] Inference coverage: inference drops BIR4 rows with any missing value (local sample: 205 of 431 scored; `price_to_close` is missing when no price was reported, `avg_target_expanding` before a ticker's first known label), although the model handles missing values natively; decide which columns a scored row must have
- [ ] Feature store on AWS: BT4/BI4/P/RT4/BIR4 rebuilt from scratch on Iceberg in S3 + Glue (the local store is a SQLite catalog in `.lakehouse/`)
- [ ] alpaca-py: smoke-test the broker adapter against the paper account (replaced alpaca-trade-api for Python 3.14)
- [x] Scraper holdings: direct and indirect holdings are kept whichever of them a filing lists (were zeroed unless both were); target adds them to the remaining shares as `total_held`
- [x] Transaction key = accession + table + position (the old accession + remaining shares + derivative key collided, ~1.5% of transactions overwritten); share and holding columns float64 (were int32/int64: truncated fractional shares, could overflow)
- [ ] Market half days: entry and exit assume a 16:00 ET close, but NYSE closes at 13:00 on a few days a year, so a filing accepted between 13:00 and 16:00 on such a day enters at a close that printed before it (look-ahead); take session times from an exchange calendar (`exchange_calendars`); the same calendar should drive `next_print`, which treats exchange holidays as trading days: a filing keyed to a holiday's open and one keyed to the next real open both enter at that open, so one trading opportunity yields two rows with the same label
- [x] Price adjustments on incremental refresh: yahoo_connect fetches the last 30 stored days again and refetches a ticker's full history when their adjustment factors moved or a split/dividend follows them; never stores a bar before its close has printed
- [ ] `total_held` across filings: traded and untraded accounts are disjoint within one filing, not across them; when an insider files twice before one print, an account traded in one filing and listed as a holding in the other is counted twice (holdings are summed per D/I, not per account, so they cannot be matched)
- [ ] AWS migration: under way on `feat/aws-migration`, from `master` at 0.4

